/-
  BlindPRF/PRFSecurity.lean — the "pseudorandom function" in the design.

  The signature party A obtains is `prf H k m = k • H m`.  The classical claim is: with the hash
  `H` modelled as a random oracle, the keyed function `m ↦ k • H m` is a pseudorandom function
  under the Decisional Diffie–Hellman (DDH) assumption.

  HONESTY POINT FIRST.  Hardness cannot be proved.  DDH is an ASSUMPTION; it is neither proved
  nor postulated as an axiom anywhere in this file.  What is proved is the REDUCTION, and it is
  proved as exact equalities between explicitly defined experiments (probability distributions):
  whatever a distinguisher achieves against the PRF, an explicitly written DDH distinguisher
  built from it achieves against DDH.  Section J shows how much room that leaves: with a single
  message the value is statistically pseudorandom (every distinguisher has advantage at most
  `1/|F|`), whereas with two or more messages (and `g ≠ 0`) an explicit brute-force test has
  advantage at least `1 - 1/|F|`, so there the PRF property can at best be computational.

  The PRF game.  The message space `M` is finite and the distinguisher is handed the ENTIRE hash
  table `m ↦ H m` together with the ENTIRE table of function values, i.e. a pair of tables
  `Tables M G = (M → G) × (M → G)`:

  * `prfReal F g`  — hash table `m ↦ h m • g` for uniform exponents `h`, with the true values
                     `m ↦ k • H m` under a uniform key `k`;
  * `prfRand F g`  — the same kind of hash table, with independent uniform multiples of `g`
                     instead (independent uniform group elements when `g` is a generator).

  An adaptive oracle adversary is a special case of such a distinguisher (section I).

  The DDH experiments over the group generated by `g` are

  * `ddhReal F g`   — `(a • g, b • g, (a * b) • g)` for uniform `a, b`;
  * `ddhRand F g`   — `(a • g, b • g, c • g)` for uniform `a, b, c` (textbook DDH);
  * `ddhRandNe F g` — the same with `(a, b, c)` uniform among the triples with `c ≠ a * b`.

  Contents.

  * 0. General facts about uniform sampling (marginals of uniform functions, a counting bound).
  * A. The experiments and the reduction `reduction F g : G × G × G → PMF (Tables M G)`, the
       classical random self-reduction: from `(A, B, C)` build the hash table `x • B + y • g`
       and the value table `x • C + y • A` with fresh uniform `x, y : M → F`.  Its deterministic
       core `reductionCore` is a plain function that does not use `Classical.choice`.
  * B. Sanity: `prfReal` really is "uniform key, uniformly random hash function `H : M → G`,
       output `(H, prf H k)`", and `prfRand` really is the uniform pair of tables (both need a
       generator); `ddhRand` is the uniform triple of group elements.
  * C. `reduction_real`, `reduction_rand`: the reduction maps `ddhReal` EXACTLY onto `prfReal`
       and `ddhRandNe` EXACTLY onto `prfRand`.  No generator hypothesis is needed.
  * D. Consequences for an arbitrary distinguisher `D`: the PRF experiments with `D` ARE the DDH
       experiments with the explicit distinguisher `ddhDistinguisher F g D`.
  * E. The `c ≠ a * b` variant versus textbook DDH: `ddhRand` is EXACTLY the mixture "with
       probability `1/|F|` a real DH triple, otherwise a uniform non-DH triple"; consequently
       all outcome probabilities of the two experiments differ by at most `1/|F|`.
  * F. Advantages: `PRF-advantage(D) = DDH≠-advantage(reduction, then D)`,
       `(|F| - 1) * PRF-advantage(D) = |F| * DDH-advantage(reduction, then D)`, hence
       `PRF-advantage(D) ≤ DDH-advantage(reduction, then D) + 1/|F|`.
  * G. The same reduction when the distinguisher is ALSO given the public key `k • g`.
  * H. Locality: restricting the tables to any set of messages commutes with the reduction and
       with both PRF experiments.  A distinguisher reading `|S|` fixed entries is served by a
       reduction that builds `2 * |S|` entries, whatever the size of `M`.
  * I. Adaptive oracle adversaries (decision trees) and lazy sampling: the reduction that
       answers queries one at a time, drawing coins only for the messages asked, is EXACTLY the
       table-building reduction followed by the adversary.  Also for randomised adversaries and
       for adversaries that see the public key.
  * J. What unbounded distinguishers achieve (one message: at most `1/|F|`; two or more: at
       least `1 - 1/|F|`), and non-degeneracy of the PRF and DDH games.
  * K. Non-vacuity: a model of all hypotheses.

  What is NOT claimed or modelled:

  * DDH (or any other hardness assumption) is not proved and not assumed.  No theorem here says
    that the advantage of an efficient distinguisher is small.
  * The random oracle is modelled by sampling the hash table uniformly: `H m = h m • g` with
    uniform exponents `h` (shown in section B to be a uniformly random function into the group;
    cf. `progHash_uniform` in `KeyPrivacy.lean`).  No statement is made about any concrete hash.
  * COST.  Lean has no cost model here, so no theorem mentions running time.  What can be read
    off the definitions: `reduction` (section A) builds both complete tables, i.e. it performs
    `2 * |M|` double scalar multiplications.  For a message space small enough to tabulate that
    is (by inspection) an efficient DDH distinguisher whenever `D` is.  For a realistic message
    space (`|M|` around `2^256`) it is NOT, and the theorems of sections C to G alone would
    transfer nothing from DDH.  The transfer for large `M` rests on lazy sampling, which IS
    proved: for a set of queries fixed in advance in section H, and for adaptive queries in
    section I, where `lazyDistinguisher` computes two table entries per distinct query and is
    proved equal to the table-building distinguisher.  What remains by inspection: that
    `reductionEntry` and `OracleAdv.lazyRun` are cheap, and that they take neither a key nor a
    discrete logarithm.
  * Adversaries in section I are decision trees, i.e. arbitrary functions of the answers seen so
    far; there is no machine model and no bound on the number of queries.  The theorems hold for
    every tree, so in particular for the efficient ones, but "efficient" is not formalised.
  * The distinguisher sees hash values and function values (and, in section G, the public key).
    It is NOT given an oracle `a ↦ k • a` for group elements `a` of its choice.  Party A of the
    protocol has exactly such an oracle (`evaluate` is applied to whatever B receives), so
    nothing in this file bounds a party A that deviates from the protocol.  That needs a
    one-more-Diffie–Hellman type assumption; see section L4 of `Limitations.lean` and
    `KeyPrivacy.lean`.
  * The reduction lands exactly on the `c ≠ a * b` variant of DDH; that variant differs from
    textbook DDH by statistical distance at most `1/|F|` (section E), which is where the additive
    `1/|F|` in section F comes from.
  * `M` must be finite, because the games are defined by sampling whole tables.  It may be
    arbitrarily large (sections H and I), but an infinite message space such as "all bit
    strings" is not modelled; bound the message length first.
  * `F` may be any finite field.  The statements are Diffie–Hellman statements about a GROUP
    only for a prime field `F = ZMod q` (then scalar multiplication is repeated addition and
    `(a * b) • g` is the Diffie–Hellman value of `a • g` and `b • g`); for other finite fields
    they are true but are not statements about a group.
  * This file is about the pseudorandomness of the function values only.  It says nothing about
    the interactive protocol (blinding, a malicious A; see the other files).  It is also about
    the pairing-free setting: with the optional pairing layer of `PublicVerify.lean`, anyone
    holding the public key in `G₂` can VERIFY signatures, so there the values are deliberately
    not pseudorandom to such a party (and DDH in a group with a symmetric pairing is false).

  Everything lives in the namespace `BlindPRF.PRFSecurity`.
-/
import BlindPRF.Correctness
import BlindPRF.Prob

namespace BlindPRF

namespace PRFSecurity

open scoped ENNReal

universe u v w x y z

/-! ## 0. General facts about uniform sampling (not specific to the protocol) -/

section General

variable {α : Type u} {β : Type v} {γ : Type w}

/-- Sampling `a`, then sampling `b` and outputting `f a b`, is the same as sampling the pair
`(a, b)` uniformly and outputting `f a b`. -/
theorem unif_bind_map [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] [Fintype β] [Nonempty β] (f : α → β → γ) :
    ((unif α).bind fun a => (unif β).map (f a)) = (unif (α × β)).map fun p => f p.1 p.2 := by
  rw [← PMF.bind_pure_comp, unif_prod_bind]
  rfl

/-- Probability of an outcome after a uniform first step, as a finite sum. -/
theorem unif_bind_apply [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] (f : α → PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    (unif α).bind f c = ∑ a, (Fintype.card α : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * f a c := by
  rw [PMF.bind_apply, tsum_fintype]
  rfl

/-- Every point has positive probability under the uniform distribution. -/
theorem mem_support_unif [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] (a : α) : a ∈ (unif α).support := by
  rw [PMF.mem_support_iff, unif_apply]
  exact ENNReal.inv_ne_zero.mpr (ENNReal.natCast_ne_top _)

/-- Averaging a constant over a uniform sample gives the constant. -/
theorem sum_unif_const [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] (c : ℝ≥0∞) :
    ∑ _a : α, (Fintype.card α : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * c = c := by
  rw [Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_univ, nsmul_eq_mul, ← mul_assoc,
    ENNReal.mul_inv_cancel (by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_ne_zero) (ENNReal.natCast_ne_top _),
    one_mul]

end General

section Marginals

variable {α : Type*} {β : Type*} {α' : Type*} {β' : Type*}

/-- **Product rule for marginals.**  If `π` pushes the uniform distribution on `α` to the uniform
distribution on `α'`, and `ρ` does the same from `β` to `β'`, then `Prod.map π ρ` pushes the
uniform distribution on `α × β` to the uniform distribution on `α' × β'`. -/
theorem unif_map_prodMap [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] [Fintype β] [Nonempty β] [Fintype α']
    [Nonempty α'] [Fintype β'] [Nonempty β'] (π : α → α') (ρ : β → β')
    (hπ : (unif α).map π = unif α') (hρ : (unif β).map ρ = unif β') :
    (unif (α × β)).map (Prod.map π ρ) = unif (α' × β') := by
  rw [unif_prod, PMF.map_bind, unif_prod, ← hπ, PMF.bind_map]
  congr 1
  funext a
  rw [Function.comp_apply, ← hρ, PMF.map_comp, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

variable {S : Type*} {M : Type*} {K : Type*}

/-- **Marginal of a uniform function.**  Restricting a uniformly random function `M → K` along an
injection `ι : S → M` gives a uniformly random function `S → K`: the values at the points of `S`
are uniform, whatever happens at the other points. -/
theorem unif_map_comp_injective [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]
    [Fintype K] [Nonempty K] (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) :
    (unif (M → K)).map (fun x => x ∘ ι) = unif (S → K) := by
  classical
  let e₁ : S ⊕ ↥(Set.range ι)ᶜ ≃ M :=
    (Equiv.sumCongr (Equiv.ofInjective ι hι) (Equiv.refl _)).trans
      (Equiv.Set.sumCompl (Set.range ι))
  let e : (M → K) ≃ (S → K) × (↥(Set.range ι)ᶜ → K) :=
    (Equiv.arrowCongr e₁.symm (Equiv.refl K)).trans (Equiv.sumArrowEquivProdArrow _ _ _)
  refine unif_map_of_equiv_prod e _ fun x => ?_
  funext s
  simp [e, e₁, Equiv.sumArrowEquivProdArrow, Equiv.arrowCongr]

/-- **Resampling one coordinate.**  Overwriting one value of a uniformly random function by an
independent uniform value gives a uniformly random function. -/
theorem unif_map_update [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] [Fintype K] [Nonempty K] (m : M) :
    (unif (K × (M → K))).map (fun p => Function.update p.2 m p.1) = unif (M → K) := by
  let e : K × (M → K) ≃ (M → K) × K :=
    { toFun := fun p => (Function.update p.2 m p.1, p.2 m)
      invFun := fun q => (q.1 m, Function.update q.1 m q.2)
      left_inv := by
        rintro ⟨v, c⟩
        simp
      right_inv := by
        rintro ⟨c, w⟩
        simp }
  exact unif_map_of_equiv_prod e _ fun _ => rfl

/-- **Counting bound.**  If every sample accepted by the test `t` is hit by `f : κ → α`, then a
uniform sample is accepted with probability at most `|κ| / |α|`. -/
theorem unif_bind_pure_true_le {κ : Type*} [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] [Fintype κ]
    (t : α → Bool) (f : κ → α) (hf : ∀ a, t a = true → ∃ c, f c = a) :
    ((unif α).bind fun a => PMF.pure (t a)) true ≤
      (Fintype.card κ : ℝ≥0∞) / (Fintype.card α : ℝ≥0∞) := by
  classical
  have h1 : ∀ a, (Fintype.card α : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * (PMF.pure (t a)) true =
      if t a = true then (Fintype.card α : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ else 0 := by
    intro a
    by_cases h : t a = true <;> simp [h]
  have h2 : (Finset.univ.filter fun a => t a = true).card ≤ Fintype.card κ :=
    calc (Finset.univ.filter fun a => t a = true).card ≤ (Finset.univ.image f).card :=
          Finset.card_le_card fun a ha => by
            obtain ⟨c, rfl⟩ := hf a (Finset.mem_filter.mp ha).2
            exact Finset.mem_image_of_mem f (Finset.mem_univ c)
      _ ≤ Fintype.card κ := Finset.card_image_le
  rw [unif_bind_apply]
  simp only [h1]
  rw [← Finset.sum_filter, Finset.sum_const, nsmul_eq_mul, div_eq_mul_inv]
  gcongr

end Marginals

variable {F : Type u} {G : Type v} {M : Type w}

/-! ## A. The experiments and the reduction -/

/-- What the PRF distinguisher is handed: the entire hash table `m ↦ H m` (first component) and
an entire table of candidate function values (second component). -/
abbrev Tables (M : Type w) (G : Type v) : Type (max v w) := (M → G) × (M → G)

/-- Triples of scalars `(a, b, c)` with `c ≠ a * b`: the exponents of the non-Diffie–Hellman
triples `(a • g, b • g, c • g)`. -/
abbrev NonDH (F : Type u) [Field F] : Type u := {t : F × F × F // t.2.2 ≠ t.1 * t.2.1}

/-- There is a non-DH triple of exponents, namely `(0, 0, 1)`, because `0 ≠ 1` in a field. -/
instance instNonemptyNonDH [Field F] : Nonempty (NonDH F) := ⟨⟨(0, 0, 1), by simp⟩⟩

section Cores

variable [Field F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G]

/-- One (hash, value) pair of table entries of the reduction, computed from the instance
`inst = (A, B, C)` and the two coins `(x, y)` of one message: `(x • B + y • g, x • C + y • A)`.
Two double scalar multiplications; no key, no discrete logarithm. -/
def reductionEntry (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) (xy : F × F) : G × G :=
  (xy.1 • inst.2.1 + xy.2 • g, xy.1 • inst.2.2 + xy.2 • inst.1)

/-- **The deterministic core of the reduction**: instance `inst = (A, B, C)` and coin tables
`(x, y)` in, pair of tables out.

* hash table  `m ↦ x m • B + y m • g`,
* value table `m ↦ x m • C + y m • A`.

This is a plain function of group elements and coins.  It is defined without the uniform
distribution and (as `#print axioms` shows) without `Classical.choice`, so it cannot be hiding a
discrete logarithm taken through the classical inverse of `c ↦ c • g`; it is the object to list
under the project's `#audit_choice_free` check.  `reduction` is its pushforward under uniform
coins. -/
def reductionCore (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) (xy : (M → F) × (M → F)) : Tables M G :=
  (fun m => xy.1 m • inst.2.1 + xy.2 m • g, fun m => xy.1 m • inst.2.2 + xy.2 m • inst.1)

/-- The tables of `reductionCore` are built entry by entry: the entries at `m` depend only on the
two coins `x m, y m` of that message. -/
theorem reductionCore_entry (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) (xy : (M → F) × (M → F)) (m : M) :
    ((reductionCore g inst xy).1 m, (reductionCore g inst xy).2 m) =
      reductionEntry g inst (xy.1 m, xy.2 m) :=
  rfl

end Cores

section Defs

variable (F) [Field F] [Fintype F] [DecidableEq F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G]
  [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]

/-- **PRF game, real world.**  Sample a key `k : F` and a table of exponents `h : M → F`
uniformly; output the random-oracle hash table `H = (m ↦ h m • g)` together with the true
function table `m ↦ k • H m = prf H k m` (see `prfReal_eq` and `prfReal_eq_randomOracle`). -/
noncomputable def prfReal (g : G) : PMF (Tables M G) :=
  (unif (F × (M → F))).map fun kh => (fun m => kh.2 m • g, fun m => kh.1 • (kh.2 m • g))

/-- **PRF game, random world.**  The same random-oracle hash table, together with a table of
independent uniform multiples of `g` (independent uniform group elements when `g` is a generator,
see `prfRand_eq_unif`). -/
noncomputable def prfRand (g : G) : PMF (Tables M G) :=
  (unif ((M → F) × (M → F))).map fun hu => (fun m => hu.1 m • g, fun m => hu.2 m • g)

/-- **DDH, real world**: a Diffie–Hellman triple `(a • g, b • g, (a * b) • g)`, `a, b` uniform. -/
noncomputable def ddhReal (g : G) : PMF (G × G × G) :=
  (unif (F × F)).map fun ab => (ab.1 • g, ab.2 • g, (ab.1 * ab.2) • g)

/-- **DDH, random world, `c ≠ a * b` variant**: `(a • g, b • g, c • g)` with `(a, b, c)` uniform
among the exponent triples that are NOT Diffie–Hellman triples.  This is the variant onto which
the reduction maps exactly; section E relates it to the textbook `ddhRand`. -/
noncomputable def ddhRandNe (g : G) : PMF (G × G × G) :=
  (unif (NonDH F)).map fun t => (t.1.1 • g, t.1.2.1 • g, t.1.2.2 • g)

/-- **DDH, random world, textbook**: `(a • g, b • g, c • g)` with `a, b, c` uniform. -/
noncomputable def ddhRand (g : G) : PMF (G × G × G) :=
  (unif (F × F × F)).map fun t => (t.1 • g, t.2.1 • g, t.2.2 • g)

/-- **The reduction** (random self-reduction of Diffie–Hellman triples).  Input: a triple
`inst = (A, B, C)` of group elements.  Sample `x, y : M → F` uniformly and output
`reductionCore g inst (x, y)`, i.e.

* the hash table  `m ↦ x m • B + y m • g`,
* the value table `m ↦ x m • C + y m • A`.

The inputs are group elements only: no key and no discrete logarithm.

COST, stated honestly.  As written this builds BOTH COMPLETE TABLES: two double scalar
multiplications for every message, i.e. work linear in `|M|`.  That is feasible only for a
message space small enough to tabulate.  For a large message space (say `|M| ≈ 2^256`) the
reduction has to be run lazily, building only the entries the distinguisher reads.  That this
changes nothing is a theorem, not an inspection: section H for a fixed set of queried messages
(`reduction_restrict`, `ddhDistinguisher_restrict`) and section I for adaptive oracle adversaries
(`OracleAdv.lazyRun_eq_reduction`).  That either form is "efficient" is by inspection of the
definitions; Lean has no cost model here. -/
noncomputable def reduction (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) : PMF (Tables M G) :=
  (unif ((M → F) × (M → F))).map (reductionCore g inst)

/-- The DDH distinguisher built from a PRF distinguisher `D`: on input a triple, run the
reduction to produce a pair of tables and hand them to `D`.  It uses `D` as a black box, once.

Because `D` takes complete tables, this distinguisher does work linear in `|M|` on top of one
run of `D`; see the docstring of `reduction`, and `lazyDistinguisher` for the version whose work
is proportional to the number of oracle queries instead. -/
noncomputable def ddhDistinguisher {β : Type x} (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF β) :
    G × G × G → PMF β :=
  fun inst => (reduction F g inst).bind D

end Defs

/-! ## B. Sanity: the experiments are what they are meant to be -/

section Sanity

variable [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]

/-- The value table of the real PRF experiment is literally `m ↦ prf H k m`, where `H` is the
sampled hash table `m ↦ h m • g`.  (True by definition; this ties `prfReal` to `BlindPRF.prf`.) -/
theorem prfReal_eq (g : G) :
    (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)) =
      (unif (F × (M → F))).map fun kh =>
        ((fun m => kh.2 m • g, fun m => prf (fun m' => kh.2 m' • g) kh.1 m) : Tables M G) :=
  rfl

/-- **The real PRF experiment in the random-oracle model.**  If `g` generates the group, then
`prfReal F g` is exactly: sample a uniform key `k`, sample a uniformly random hash FUNCTION
`H : M → G`, and output `H` together with the function table `m ↦ prf H k m`.

So programming the oracle through exponents loses nothing.  This is a statement about
distributions; it says nothing about any concrete hash function. -/
theorem prfReal_eq_randomOracle [Fintype G] {g : G} (hg : IsGenerator F g) :
    (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)) =
      (unif F).bind fun k => (unif (M → G)).map fun H => (H, fun m => prf H k m) := by
  rw [unif_bind_map]
  exact unif_map_eq_of_equiv
    (Equiv.prodCongr (Equiv.refl F) (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : M => hg.equiv)) _ _ fun _ => rfl

/-- **The random PRF experiment is the uniform pair of tables.**  If `g` generates the group,
then `prfRand F g` is exactly the uniform distribution on pairs (hash table, value table): a
uniformly random hash function together with independent uniformly random group elements. -/
theorem prfRand_eq_unif [Fintype G] {g : G} (hg : IsGenerator F g) :
    (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)) = unif (Tables M G) :=
  unif_map_equiv (Equiv.prodCongr (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : M => hg.equiv)
    (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : M => hg.equiv))

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- The textbook random DDH experiment is the uniform triple of group elements, if `g` generates
the group. -/
theorem ddhRand_eq_unif [Fintype G] {g : G} (hg : IsGenerator F g) :
    ddhRand F g = unif (G × G × G) :=
  unif_map_equiv (Equiv.prodCongr hg.equiv (Equiv.prodCongr hg.equiv hg.equiv))

end Sanity

/-! ## C. The reduction is exact -/

section Reduction

variable [Field F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G]

/-- An entry of the reduction's hash table, in terms of exponents. -/
theorem hash_entry (g : G) (x y b : F) : x • b • g + y • g = (x * b + y) • g := by
  rw [add_smul, mul_smul]

/-- An entry of the reduction's value table, in terms of exponents. -/
theorem value_entry (g : G) (x y a c : F) : x • c • g + y • a • g = (x * c + y * a) • g := by
  rw [add_smul, mul_smul, mul_smul]

/-- On a Diffie–Hellman triple the value entry is the key `a` times the hash entry. -/
theorem value_entry_real (g : G) (x y a b : F) :
    x • (a * b) • g + y • a • g = a • (x * b + y) • g := by
  rw [value_entry, smul_smul]
  congr 1
  ring

/-- The relabelling of the sample space used in the real case:
`((a, b), (x, y)) ↦ ((a, x * b + y), (b, x))`.  Its first component is (key, hash exponents). -/
def realEquiv (F : Type u) (M : Type w) [Field F] :
    (F × F) × ((M → F) × (M → F)) ≃ (F × (M → F)) × (F × (M → F)) where
  toFun p := ((p.1.1, fun m => p.2.1 m * p.1.2 + p.2.2 m), (p.1.2, p.2.1))
  invFun q := ((q.1.1, q.2.1), (q.2.2, fun m => q.1.2 m - q.2.2 m * q.2.1))
  left_inv := by
    rintro ⟨⟨a, b⟩, ⟨x, y⟩⟩
    simp
  right_inv := by
    rintro ⟨⟨k, h⟩, ⟨b, x⟩⟩
    simp

/-- The relabelling of the reduction's coins used in the random case: for a fixed non-DH triple
of exponents (`c ≠ a * b`) the map `(x, y) ↦ (x * b + y, x * c + y * a)` is a bijection, because
its determinant `a * b - c` is nonzero.  It is applied entrywise to the two tables. -/
def randEquiv (M : Type w) {a b c : F} (h : c ≠ a * b) :
    ((M → F) × (M → F)) ≃ ((M → F) × (M → F)) where
  toFun xy := (fun m => xy.1 m * b + xy.2 m, fun m => xy.1 m * c + xy.2 m * a)
  invFun hu := (fun m => (hu.2 m - hu.1 m * a) / (c - a * b),
    fun m => hu.1 m - (hu.2 m - hu.1 m * a) / (c - a * b) * b)
  left_inv := by
    rintro ⟨x, y⟩
    have hd : c - a * b ≠ 0 := sub_ne_zero.mpr h
    have hx : ∀ m, (x m * c + y m * a - (x m * b + y m) * a) / (c - a * b) = x m := by
      intro m
      rw [div_eq_iff hd]
      ring
    ext m
    · exact hx m
    · show x m * b + y m - (x m * c + y m * a - (x m * b + y m) * a) / (c - a * b) * b = y m
      rw [hx m]
      ring
  right_inv := by
    rintro ⟨h', u⟩
    have hd : c - a * b ≠ 0 := sub_ne_zero.mpr h
    have hx : ∀ m, (u m - h' m * a) / (c - a * b) * (c - a * b) = u m - h' m * a :=
      fun m => div_mul_cancel₀ _ hd
    ext m
    · show (u m - h' m * a) / (c - a * b) * b
        + (h' m - (u m - h' m * a) / (c - a * b) * b) = h' m
      ring
    · show (u m - h' m * a) / (c - a * b) * c
        + (h' m - (u m - h' m * a) / (c - a * b) * b) * a = u m
      linear_combination hx m

variable [Fintype F] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]

/-- **The reduction, real case.**  Feeding a uniformly random Diffie–Hellman triple
`(a • g, b • g, (a * b) • g)` to the reduction produces EXACTLY the real PRF experiment: the
key is `a`, and the hash exponents `x m * b + y m` are uniform and independent of the key.

This is an equality of distributions on pairs of tables, for every module `G` and every `g`
(no generator hypothesis).  It is one half of the reduction; it does not say DDH is hard, and
it says nothing about the running time of `reduction` (read its definition). -/
theorem reduction_real (g : G) :
    (ddhReal F g).bind (reduction F g) = (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)) := by
  have h1 : (ddhReal F g).bind (reduction F g (M := M)) =
      (unif ((F × F) × ((M → F) × (M → F)))).map fun p =>
        ((fun m => p.2.1 m • p.1.2 • g + p.2.2 m • g,
          fun m => p.2.1 m • (p.1.1 * p.1.2) • g + p.2.2 m • p.1.1 • g) : Tables M G) := by
    unfold ddhReal
    rw [PMF.bind_map]
    exact unif_bind_map fun (ab : F × F) (xy : (M → F) × (M → F)) =>
      ((fun m => xy.1 m • ab.2 • g + xy.2 m • g,
        fun m => xy.1 m • (ab.1 * ab.2) • g + xy.2 m • ab.1 • g) : Tables M G)
  have h2 : (fun p : (F × F) × ((M → F) × (M → F)) =>
        ((fun m => p.2.1 m • p.1.2 • g + p.2.2 m • g,
          fun m => p.2.1 m • (p.1.1 * p.1.2) • g + p.2.2 m • p.1.1 • g) : Tables M G)) =
      (fun kh : F × (M → F) =>
          ((fun m => kh.2 m • g, fun m => kh.1 • (kh.2 m • g)) : Tables M G)) ∘
        fun p => (p.1.1, fun m => p.2.1 m * p.1.2 + p.2.2 m) := by
    funext p
    simp only [Function.comp_apply, hash_entry, value_entry_real]
  rw [h1, h2, ← PMF.map_comp, unif_map_of_equiv_prod (realEquiv F M)
    (fun p => (p.1.1, fun m => p.2.1 m * p.1.2 + p.2.2 m)) fun _ => rfl]
  rfl

/-- On ANY fixed non-Diffie–Hellman triple (`c ≠ a * b`) the reduction already outputs exactly
the random PRF experiment `prfRand F g`: the entries of both tables are independent uniform
multiples of `g` (independent uniform group elements when `g` is a generator, by
`prfRand_eq_unif`; no generator hypothesis is needed for the statement itself). -/
theorem reduction_of_nonDH (g : G) {a b c : F} (h : c ≠ a * b) :
    reduction F g (a • g, b • g, c • g) = (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)) :=
  unif_map_eq_of_equiv (randEquiv M h) _ _ fun xy => by
    simp only [randEquiv, Equiv.coe_fn_mk, reductionCore, hash_entry, value_entry]

/-- **The reduction, random case.**  Feeding a uniformly random NON-Diffie–Hellman triple
(`c ≠ a * b`) to the reduction produces EXACTLY the random PRF experiment.

Again an equality of distributions, for every module `G` and every `g`.  Note the `c ≠ a * b`
variant of DDH on the left, not textbook DDH; the two differ by statistical distance at most
`1/|F|`, which is proved in section E (`ddhRand_eq_mixture`). -/
theorem reduction_rand [DecidableEq F] (g : G) :
    (ddhRandNe F g).bind (reduction F g) = (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)) := by
  have h : (reduction F g ∘ fun t : NonDH F => (t.1.1 • g, t.1.2.1 • g, t.1.2.2 • g)) =
      fun _ => (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)) := by
    funext t
    exact reduction_of_nonDH g t.2
  unfold ddhRandNe
  rw [PMF.bind_map, h, unif_bind_const]

end Reduction

/-! ## D. Consequences for distinguishers -/

section Distinguishers

variable [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]
  {β : Type x}

/-- Unfolding `ddhDistinguisher`: it is literally "run the reduction, then `D`". -/
@[simp]
theorem ddhDistinguisher_apply (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF β) (inst : G × G × G) :
    ddhDistinguisher F g D inst = (reduction F g inst).bind D :=
  rfl

/-- **Real worlds coincide.**  For every distinguisher `D` (any function from pairs of tables to
distributions over any output type; no efficiency requirement), running `D` in the real PRF
experiment is the same experiment as running the explicit DDH distinguisher
`ddhDistinguisher F g D` ("run the reduction, then `D`") on a real Diffie–Hellman triple. -/
theorem prfReal_bind_eq (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF β) :
    (prfReal F g).bind D = (ddhReal F g).bind (ddhDistinguisher F g D) := by
  rw [← reduction_real, PMF.bind_bind]
  rfl

/-- **Random worlds coincide.**  For every distinguisher `D`, running `D` in the random PRF
experiment is the same experiment as running `ddhDistinguisher F g D` on a uniformly random
non-Diffie–Hellman triple (the `c ≠ a * b` variant of DDH).

Together with `prfReal_bind_eq`: the whole output distribution of `D` in either PRF world equals
that of the explicit DDH distinguisher in the corresponding DDH world.  Neither theorem says the
two worlds are hard to tell apart. -/
theorem prfRand_bind_eq [DecidableEq F] (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF β) :
    (prfRand F g).bind D = (ddhRandNe F g).bind (ddhDistinguisher F g D) := by
  rw [← reduction_rand, PMF.bind_bind]
  rfl

end Distinguishers

/-! ## E. The `c ≠ a * b` variant versus textbook DDH -/

section SplitZero

variable {γ : Type x} [Field F] [DecidableEq F]

/-- The function underlying `zeroSwap`: if `z ≠ 0`, exchange `z` and the unit `w`. -/
def zeroSwapFun (p : F × Fˣ) : F × Fˣ :=
  if h : p.1 = 0 then p else ((p.2 : F), Units.mk0 p.1 h)

theorem zeroSwapFun_involutive : Function.Involutive (zeroSwapFun (F := F)) := by
  rintro ⟨z, w⟩
  by_cases h : z = 0
  · simp [zeroSwapFun, h]
  · simp [zeroSwapFun, h]

/-- A relabelling of `F × Fˣ` (an involution): if `z ≠ 0`, exchange `z` and the unit `w`. -/
def zeroSwap (F : Type u) [Field F] [DecidableEq F] : F × Fˣ ≃ F × Fˣ where
  toFun := zeroSwapFun
  invFun := zeroSwapFun
  left_inv := zeroSwapFun_involutive
  right_inv := zeroSwapFun_involutive

theorem zeroSwap_fst (p : F × Fˣ) : (zeroSwap F p).1 = if p.1 = 0 then 0 else (p.2 : F) := by
  show (zeroSwapFun p).1 = _
  obtain ⟨z, w⟩ := p
  by_cases h : z = 0
  · simp [zeroSwapFun, h]
  · simp [zeroSwapFun, h]

/-- **A uniform scalar is zero with probability `1/|F|` and otherwise a uniform nonzero
scalar.**  Stated for an arbitrary continuation `f`; proved by relabelling, with no arithmetic
on probabilities. -/
theorem unif_bind_split_zero [Fintype F] (f : F → PMF γ) :
    (unif F).bind f =
      (unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then f 0 else (unif Fˣ).bind fun w => f (w : F) := by
  have h1 : (fun z : F => if z = 0 then f 0 else (unif Fˣ).bind fun w => f (w : F)) =
      fun z => (unif Fˣ).bind fun w => f (zeroSwap F (z, w)).1 := by
    funext z
    by_cases hz : z = 0
    · simp [zeroSwap_fst, hz]
    · simp [zeroSwap_fst, hz]
  rw [h1, ← unif_prod_bind fun p : F × Fˣ => f (zeroSwap F p).1,
    unif_bind_eq_of_equiv (zeroSwap F) (fun p => f (zeroSwap F p).1) (fun p => f p.1)
      fun _ => rfl,
    unif_prod_bind]
  simp only [unif_bind_const]

variable [Fintype F]

/-- A "one in `|F|`" mixture of `p` into `p'` gives every outcome at most `1/|F|` more
probability than `p'` does. -/
theorem mix_apply_le (p p' : PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    ((unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then p else p') c ≤
      p' c + (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := by
  calc ((unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then p else p') c
      = ∑ z : F, (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * (if z = 0 then p else p') c :=
        unif_bind_apply _ _
    _ ≤ ∑ z : F, ((Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * p' c +
          if z = 0 then (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ else 0) := by
        refine Finset.sum_le_sum fun z _ => ?_
        by_cases hz : z = 0
        · simp only [hz, ite_true]
          calc (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * p c
              ≤ (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * 1 := by
                gcongr
                exact PMF.coe_le_one p c
            _ = (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := mul_one _
            _ ≤ _ := le_add_self
        · simp [hz]
    _ = p' c + (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := by
        rw [Finset.sum_add_distrib, sum_unif_const, Finset.sum_ite_eq']
        simp

/-- A "one in `|F|`" mixture of `p` into `p'` gives every outcome at most `1/|F|` less
probability than `p'` does. -/
theorem le_mix_apply (p p' : PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    p' c ≤ ((unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then p else p') c +
      (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := by
  calc p' c = ∑ _z : F, (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * p' c := (sum_unif_const _).symm
    _ ≤ ∑ z : F, ((Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * (if z = 0 then p else p') c +
          if z = 0 then (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ else 0) := by
        refine Finset.sum_le_sum fun z _ => ?_
        by_cases hz : z = 0
        · simp only [hz, ite_true]
          calc (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * p' c
              ≤ (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * 1 := by
                gcongr
                exact PMF.coe_le_one p' c
            _ = (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := mul_one _
            _ ≤ _ := le_add_self
        · simp [hz]
    _ = ((unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then p else p') c +
          (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := by
        rw [Finset.sum_add_distrib, ← unif_bind_apply, Finset.sum_ite_eq']
        simp

/-- The exact weights of a "one in `|F|`" mixture, in real numbers:
`|F| * mix(c) = p(c) + (|F| - 1) * p'(c)`. -/
theorem mix_apply_toReal (p p' : PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    (Fintype.card F : ℝ) * (((unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then p else p') c).toReal =
      (p c).toReal + ((Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1) * (p' c).toReal := by
  have hq : (Fintype.card F : ℝ) ≠ 0 := by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_ne_zero
  have hterm : ∀ z : F, ((Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ * (if z = 0 then p else p') c).toReal =
      (Fintype.card F : ℝ)⁻¹ * (p' c).toReal +
        if z = 0 then (Fintype.card F : ℝ)⁻¹ * ((p c).toReal - (p' c).toReal) else 0 := by
    intro z
    by_cases hz : z = 0
    · simp only [hz, ite_true, ENNReal.toReal_mul, ENNReal.toReal_inv, ENNReal.toReal_natCast]
      ring
    · simp only [hz, ite_false, ENNReal.toReal_mul, ENNReal.toReal_inv, ENNReal.toReal_natCast,
        add_zero]
  rw [unif_bind_apply, ENNReal.toReal_sum fun z _ => ENNReal.mul_ne_top
    (ENNReal.inv_ne_top.mpr (by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_ne_zero)) (PMF.apply_ne_top _ _)]
  simp only [hterm]
  rw [Finset.sum_add_distrib, Finset.sum_const, Finset.card_univ, Finset.sum_ite_eq',
    nsmul_eq_mul]
  simp only [Finset.mem_univ, ite_true]
  field_simp
  ring

end SplitZero

section DDHVariants

variable {γ : Type x} [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G]

/-- A Diffie–Hellman triple whose third exponent is shifted by a fixed `z`:
`(a • g, b • g, (a * b + z) • g)` for uniform `a, b`. -/
noncomputable def ddhShift (g : G) (z : F) : PMF (G × G × G) :=
  (unif (F × F)).map fun ab => (ab.1 • g, ab.2 • g, (ab.1 * ab.2 + z) • g)

/-- Shift zero is the real DDH experiment. -/
theorem ddhShift_zero (g : G) : ddhShift g (0 : F) = ddhReal F g := by
  simp only [ddhShift, ddhReal, add_zero]

/-- `(z, (a, b)) ↦ (a, b, a * b + z)`: every triple of exponents is a DH triple plus a shift. -/
def shiftEquiv (F : Type u) [Field F] : F × (F × F) ≃ F × F × F where
  toFun p := (p.2.1, p.2.2, p.2.1 * p.2.2 + p.1)
  invFun t := (t.2.2 - t.1 * t.2.1, (t.1, t.2.1))
  left_inv := by
    rintro ⟨z, a, b⟩
    simp
  right_inv := by
    rintro ⟨a, b, c⟩
    simp

/-- `(w, (a, b)) ↦ (a, b, a * b + w)` for a NONZERO shift `w`: exactly the non-DH triples. -/
def shiftEquivNe (F : Type u) [Field F] : Fˣ × (F × F) ≃ NonDH F where
  toFun p := ⟨(p.2.1, p.2.2, p.2.1 * p.2.2 + (p.1 : F)), by simp⟩
  invFun t := (Units.mk0 (t.1.2.2 - t.1.1 * t.1.2.1) (sub_ne_zero.mpr t.2), (t.1.1, t.1.2.1))
  left_inv := by
    rintro ⟨w, a, b⟩
    ext <;> simp
  right_inv := by
    rintro ⟨⟨a, b, c⟩, h⟩
    simp

/-- Textbook random DDH = a uniformly shifted DH triple. -/
theorem ddhRand_eq_bind_shift (g : G) :
    ddhRand F g = (unif F).bind fun z => ddhShift g z := by
  unfold ddhShift ddhRand
  rw [unif_bind_map]
  exact (unif_map_eq_of_equiv (shiftEquiv F) _ _ fun _ => rfl).symm

variable [DecidableEq F]

/-- The `c ≠ a * b` variant = a DH triple shifted by a uniform NONZERO scalar. -/
theorem ddhRandNe_eq_bind_shift (g : G) :
    ddhRandNe F g = (unif Fˣ).bind fun w => ddhShift g (w : F) := by
  unfold ddhShift ddhRandNe
  rw [unif_bind_map]
  exact (unif_map_eq_of_equiv (shiftEquivNe F) _ _ fun _ => rfl).symm

/-- **Textbook DDH versus the `c ≠ a * b` variant, exactly.**  The textbook random triple
`(a • g, b • g, c • g)` is distributed as: draw a uniform scalar `z`; if `z = 0` (probability
exactly `1/|F|`) output a real Diffie–Hellman triple, otherwise output a uniformly random
non-Diffie–Hellman triple.

This is an equality of distributions (no generator hypothesis).  It is the precise sense in which
`ddhRandNe` differs from `ddhRand` "by statistical distance `1/|F|`"; quantitative consequences
are `ddhRand_bind_apply_le`, `ddhRandNe_bind_apply_le` and `card_mul_ddhAdvantage`. -/
theorem ddhRand_eq_mixture (g : G) :
    ddhRand F g = (unif F).bind fun z => if z = 0 then ddhReal F g else ddhRandNe F g := by
  rw [ddhRand_eq_bind_shift, unif_bind_split_zero, ddhShift_zero, ← ddhRandNe_eq_bind_shift]

/-- The mixture identity after an arbitrary distinguisher / post-processing `D`. -/
theorem ddhRand_bind_eq_mixture (g : G) (D : G × G × G → PMF γ) :
    (ddhRand F g).bind D =
      (unif F).bind fun z =>
        if z = 0 then (ddhReal F g).bind D else (ddhRandNe F g).bind D := by
  rw [ddhRand_eq_mixture, PMF.bind_bind]
  congr 1
  funext z
  split_ifs <;> rfl

/-- **The two random DDH experiments are `1/|F|`-close, first half.**  For every distinguisher
`D` (any output type) and every outcome `c`, the probability of `c` on a textbook random triple
exceeds the probability of `c` on a random non-DH triple by at most `1/|F|`. -/
theorem ddhRand_bind_apply_le (g : G) (D : G × G × G → PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    ((ddhRand F g).bind D) c ≤ ((ddhRandNe F g).bind D) c + (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := by
  rw [ddhRand_bind_eq_mixture]
  exact mix_apply_le _ _ _

/-- **The two random DDH experiments are `1/|F|`-close, second half.**  For every distinguisher
`D` and every outcome `c`, the probability of `c` on a random non-DH triple exceeds the
probability of `c` on a textbook random triple by at most `1/|F|`.

With `D` Boolean-valued and `c = true` the two halves say: acceptance probabilities differ by at
most `1/|F|`.  (Taking `D = pure ∘ indicator` covers every event on triples.) -/
theorem ddhRandNe_bind_apply_le (g : G) (D : G × G × G → PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    ((ddhRandNe F g).bind D) c ≤ ((ddhRand F g).bind D) c + (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞)⁻¹ := by
  rw [ddhRand_bind_eq_mixture]
  exact le_mix_apply _ _ _

/-- The exact relation between the three DDH outcome probabilities, in real numbers:
`|F| * P_rand(c) = P_real(c) + (|F| - 1) * P_ne(c)`. -/
theorem card_mul_ddhRand_bind_apply_toReal (g : G) (D : G × G × G → PMF γ) (c : γ) :
    (Fintype.card F : ℝ) * (((ddhRand F g).bind D) c).toReal =
      (((ddhReal F g).bind D) c).toReal +
        ((Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1) * (((ddhRandNe F g).bind D) c).toReal := by
  rw [ddhRand_bind_eq_mixture]
  exact mix_apply_toReal _ _ _

end DDHVariants

/-! ## F. Advantages -/

section Advantage

/-- The distinguishing advantage between two experiments with a one-bit outcome: the absolute
difference of the probabilities of outputting `true`, as a real number in `[0, 1]`. -/
noncomputable def advantage (p q : PMF Bool) : ℝ := |(p true).toReal - (q true).toReal|

theorem advantage_nonneg (p q : PMF Bool) : 0 ≤ advantage p q := abs_nonneg _

theorem advantage_le_one (p q : PMF Bool) : advantage p q ≤ 1 := by
  have h : ∀ r : PMF Bool, 0 ≤ (r true).toReal ∧ (r true).toReal ≤ 1 := fun r =>
    ⟨ENNReal.toReal_nonneg, ENNReal.toReal_le_of_le_ofReal zero_le_one
      (by simpa using PMF.coe_le_one r true)⟩
  unfold advantage
  rw [abs_le]
  constructor <;> linarith [(h p).1, (h p).2, (h q).1, (h q).2]

variable (F) [Field F] [Fintype F] [DecidableEq F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G]
  [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]

/-- The PRF advantage of a distinguisher `D`: how well it tells `prfReal` from `prfRand`. -/
noncomputable def prfAdvantage (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF Bool) : ℝ :=
  advantage ((prfReal F g).bind D) ((prfRand F g).bind D)

/-- The textbook DDH advantage of `D'`: how well it tells `ddhReal` from `ddhRand`. -/
noncomputable def ddhAdvantage (g : G) (D' : G × G × G → PMF Bool) : ℝ :=
  advantage ((ddhReal F g).bind D') ((ddhRand F g).bind D')

/-- The advantage of `D'` against the `c ≠ a * b` variant: `ddhReal` versus `ddhRandNe`. -/
noncomputable def ddhNeAdvantage (g : G) (D' : G × G × G → PMF Bool) : ℝ :=
  advantage ((ddhReal F g).bind D') ((ddhRandNe F g).bind D')

variable {F}

/-- **PRF advantage = DDH advantage, exactly (against the `c ≠ a * b` variant).**  For every
distinguisher `D`, its advantage against the PRF EQUALS the advantage of the explicit
distinguisher `ddhDistinguisher F g D` ("run the reduction, then `D`") in telling a real
Diffie–Hellman triple from a random non-Diffie–Hellman triple.

Tight in the ADVANTAGE: no loss factor, and the relation between the two advantages does not
depend on `|M|`.  The RUNNING TIME of `ddhDistinguisher F g D` does depend on `|M|`: it builds
`2 * |M|` table entries (see the docstring of `reduction`); for a version whose work is
proportional to the number of queries see `prfAdvantage_restrict_le` (fixed queries) and
`prfAdvantage_oracle_eq` (adaptive queries).  The theorem does not say the advantage is small;
that is the DDH assumption, which is not proved and not assumed. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_eq_ddhNeAdvantage (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g D = ddhNeAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisher F g D) := by
  unfold prfAdvantage ddhNeAdvantage
  rw [prfReal_bind_eq, prfRand_bind_eq]

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- **Textbook DDH advantage versus the `c ≠ a * b` variant, exactly**:
`|F| * ddhAdvantage = (|F| - 1) * ddhNeAdvantage`, for every distinguisher `D'`. -/
theorem card_mul_ddhAdvantage (g : G) (D' : G × G × G → PMF Bool) :
    (Fintype.card F : ℝ) * ddhAdvantage F g D' =
      ((Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1) * ddhNeAdvantage F g D' := by
  have h := card_mul_ddhRand_bind_apply_toReal (F := F) g D' true
  have hq0 : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := Nat.cast_nonneg _
  have hq1 : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1 :=
    sub_nonneg.mpr (by exact_mod_cast (Fintype.card_pos : 0 < Fintype.card F))
  unfold ddhAdvantage ddhNeAdvantage advantage
  calc (Fintype.card F : ℝ) *
        |(((ddhReal F g).bind D') true).toReal - (((ddhRand F g).bind D') true).toReal|
      = |(Fintype.card F : ℝ) * ((((ddhReal F g).bind D') true).toReal -
          (((ddhRand F g).bind D') true).toReal)| := by
        rw [abs_mul, abs_of_nonneg hq0]
    _ = |((Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1) * ((((ddhReal F g).bind D') true).toReal -
          (((ddhRandNe F g).bind D') true).toReal)| := by
        congr 1
        linear_combination (-1 : ℝ) * h
    _ = ((Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1) *
        |(((ddhReal F g).bind D') true).toReal - (((ddhRandNe F g).bind D') true).toReal| := by
        rw [abs_mul, abs_of_nonneg hq1]

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- The textbook DDH advantage never exceeds the advantage against the `c ≠ a * b` variant. -/
theorem ddhAdvantage_le_ddhNeAdvantage (g : G) (D' : G × G × G → PMF Bool) :
    ddhAdvantage F g D' ≤ ddhNeAdvantage F g D' := by
  have h := card_mul_ddhAdvantage (F := F) g D'
  have hq : (0 : ℝ) < (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_pos
  have h0 : 0 ≤ ddhNeAdvantage F g D' := advantage_nonneg _ _
  refine le_of_mul_le_mul_left ?_ hq
  rw [h, sub_one_mul]
  linarith

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- The advantage against the `c ≠ a * b` variant exceeds the textbook DDH advantage by at most
`1/|F|`, for every distinguisher `D'`. -/
theorem ddhNeAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add (g : G) (D' : G × G × G → PMF Bool) :
    ddhNeAdvantage F g D' ≤ ddhAdvantage F g D' + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  have h := card_mul_ddhAdvantage (F := F) g D'
  have hq : (0 : ℝ) < (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_pos
  have h1 : ddhNeAdvantage F g D' ≤ 1 := advantage_le_one _ _
  refine le_of_mul_le_mul_left ?_ hq
  rw [mul_add, mul_one_div_cancel hq.ne']
  linarith

/-- **PRF advantage versus textbook DDH advantage, exactly.**  For every distinguisher `D`,
`(|F| - 1) * prfAdvantage(D) = |F| * ddhAdvantage(run the reduction, then D)`.  An identity
between advantages; it says nothing about their size or about running time. -/
theorem card_sub_one_mul_prfAdvantage (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    ((Fintype.card F : ℝ) - 1) * prfAdvantage F g D =
      (Fintype.card F : ℝ) * ddhAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisher F g D) := by
  rw [prfAdvantage_eq_ddhNeAdvantage, card_mul_ddhAdvantage]

/-- **The PRF reduces to textbook DDH in the random-oracle model.**  For EVERY distinguisher `D`
(an arbitrary randomised function of the complete hash table and the complete value table; no
efficiency requirement), its advantage in telling the real function `m ↦ k • H m` from
independent uniform multiples of `g` (independent uniform group elements when `g` is a
generator, `prfRand_eq_unif`; the theorem itself needs no generator hypothesis) is at most the
textbook-DDH advantage of the explicit distinguisher `ddhDistinguisher F g D` plus `1/|F|`.

What this does NOT say.

* It does not say that either advantage is small.  That the DDH advantage of every *efficient*
  distinguisher is negligible is the DDH assumption for the group generated by `g`; it is not
  proved and not assumed here.
* "Efficient" is not formalised, and for THIS distinguisher it is true only for small message
  spaces: `ddhDistinguisher F g D` costs one run of `D` plus two double scalar multiplications
  for EVERY message, i.e. `2 * |M|` of them.  When `M` is too large to tabulate, use the lazy
  forms, which are proved equal to it: `prfAdvantage_restrict_le` (a fixed set of queried
  messages) and `prfAdvantage_oracle_le` (adaptive oracle adversaries).
* `D` is NOT given an oracle `a ↦ k • a` for group elements `a` of its choice.  Party A of the
  protocol has such an oracle, so this theorem does not bound a party A that deviates from the
  protocol; that needs a one-more-Diffie–Hellman type assumption, see section L4 of
  `Limitations.lean` and `KeyPrivacy.lean`.
* The hash is a random oracle (a uniformly sampled table, section B), not a concrete hash. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g D ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisher F g D) + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  rw [prfAdvantage_eq_ddhNeAdvantage]
  exact ddhNeAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g _

end Advantage

/-! ## G. The same with the public key in view

If B publishes `pk = k • g` (in the same group, no pairing), the distinguisher may be given `pk`
as well.  The reduction supplies `pk := A`, the first component of its DDH instance, and all
statements above carry over verbatim. -/

section PublicKey

/-- **The deterministic core of the reduction with public key**: as `reductionCore`, with the
first component `A` of the instance attached as the public key.  A plain function, defined
without `Classical.choice` (the second object to list under `#audit_choice_free`). -/
def reductionPkCore [Field F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] (g : G) (inst : G × G × G)
    (xy : (M → F) × (M → F)) : G × Tables M G :=
  (inst.1, reductionCore g inst xy)

section Defs

variable (F) [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]

/-- PRF game with public key, real world: `(k • g, H, m ↦ k • H m)`. -/
noncomputable def prfRealPk (g : G) : PMF (G × Tables M G) :=
  (unif (F × (M → F))).map fun kh =>
    (kh.1 • g, (fun m => kh.2 m • g, fun m => kh.1 • (kh.2 m • g)))

/-- PRF game with public key, random world: `(k • g, H, U)` with the value table `U` uniform and
independent of the public key and of the hash table. -/
noncomputable def prfRandPk (g : G) : PMF (G × Tables M G) :=
  (unif (F × ((M → F) × (M → F)))).map fun p =>
    (p.1 • g, (fun m => p.2.1 m • g, fun m => p.2.2 m • g))

/-- The reduction with public key: as `reduction`, and additionally output `A` as the public
key.  (See `reductionPk_eq`; the remarks on cost in the docstring of `reduction` apply.) -/
noncomputable def reductionPk (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) : PMF (G × Tables M G) :=
  (unif ((M → F) × (M → F))).map (reductionPkCore g inst)

/-- The DDH distinguisher built from a PRF-with-public-key distinguisher `D`. -/
noncomputable def ddhDistinguisherPk {β : Type x} (g : G) (D : G × Tables M G → PMF β) :
    G × G × G → PMF β :=
  fun inst => (reductionPk F g inst).bind D

/-- The PRF advantage of `D` when `D` is also given the public key. -/
noncomputable def prfPkAdvantage (g : G) (D : G × Tables M G → PMF Bool) : ℝ :=
  advantage ((prfRealPk F g).bind D) ((prfRandPk F g).bind D)

end Defs

variable [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]
  {β : Type x}

/-- `reductionPk` is `reduction` with the first component of the instance attached. -/
theorem reductionPk_eq (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) :
    (reductionPk F g inst : PMF (G × Tables M G)) =
      (reduction F g inst).map fun T => (inst.1, T) := by
  unfold reductionPk reduction
  rw [PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- Forgetting the public key in the real world gives `prfReal`. -/
theorem prfRealPk_map_snd (g : G) :
    (prfRealPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)).map Prod.snd = prfReal F g := by
  unfold prfRealPk prfReal
  rw [PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- Forgetting the public key in the random world gives `prfRand`. -/
theorem prfRandPk_map_snd (g : G) :
    (prfRandPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)).map Prod.snd = prfRand F g := by
  unfold prfRandPk prfRand
  rw [PMF.map_comp]
  have h : (Prod.snd ∘ fun p : F × ((M → F) × (M → F)) =>
        (p.1 • g, ((fun m => p.2.1 m • g, fun m => p.2.2 m • g) : Tables M G))) =
      (fun hu : (M → F) × (M → F) =>
        ((fun m => hu.1 m • g, fun m => hu.2 m • g) : Tables M G)) ∘ Prod.snd := rfl
  rw [h, ← PMF.map_comp, unif_map_snd]

/-- With a generator, the random world with public key is the uniform distribution on
(public key, hash table, value table). -/
theorem prfRandPk_eq_unif [Fintype G] {g : G} (hg : IsGenerator F g) :
    (prfRandPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)) = unif (G × Tables M G) :=
  unif_map_equiv (Equiv.prodCongr hg.equiv
    (Equiv.prodCongr (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : M => hg.equiv)
      (Equiv.piCongrRight fun _ : M => hg.equiv)))

/-- **The reduction with public key, real case**: on a random Diffie–Hellman triple,
`reductionPk` produces exactly the real PRF experiment including the public key `k • g`. -/
theorem reductionPk_real (g : G) :
    (ddhReal F g).bind (reductionPk F g) = (prfRealPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)) := by
  have h1 : (ddhReal F g).bind (reductionPk F g (M := M)) =
      (unif ((F × F) × ((M → F) × (M → F)))).map fun p =>
        ((p.1.1 • g, (fun m => p.2.1 m • p.1.2 • g + p.2.2 m • g,
          fun m => p.2.1 m • (p.1.1 * p.1.2) • g + p.2.2 m • p.1.1 • g)) : G × Tables M G) := by
    unfold ddhReal
    rw [PMF.bind_map]
    exact unif_bind_map fun (ab : F × F) (xy : (M → F) × (M → F)) =>
      ((ab.1 • g, (fun m => xy.1 m • ab.2 • g + xy.2 m • g,
        fun m => xy.1 m • (ab.1 * ab.2) • g + xy.2 m • ab.1 • g)) : G × Tables M G)
  have h2 : (fun p : (F × F) × ((M → F) × (M → F)) =>
        ((p.1.1 • g, (fun m => p.2.1 m • p.1.2 • g + p.2.2 m • g,
          fun m => p.2.1 m • (p.1.1 * p.1.2) • g + p.2.2 m • p.1.1 • g)) : G × Tables M G)) =
      (fun kh : F × (M → F) =>
          ((kh.1 • g, (fun m => kh.2 m • g, fun m => kh.1 • (kh.2 m • g))) : G × Tables M G)) ∘
        fun p => (p.1.1, fun m => p.2.1 m * p.1.2 + p.2.2 m) := by
    funext p
    simp only [Function.comp_apply, hash_entry, value_entry_real]
  rw [h1, h2, ← PMF.map_comp, unif_map_of_equiv_prod (realEquiv F M)
    (fun p => (p.1.1, fun m => p.2.1 m * p.1.2 + p.2.2 m)) fun _ => rfl]
  rfl

/-- Real worlds coincide, with public key. -/
theorem prfRealPk_bind_eq (g : G) (D : G × Tables M G → PMF β) :
    (prfRealPk F g).bind D = (ddhReal F g).bind (ddhDistinguisherPk F g D) := by
  rw [← reductionPk_real, PMF.bind_bind]
  rfl

variable [DecidableEq F]

omit [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- The first exponent of a uniformly random non-DH triple is uniform. -/
theorem nonDH_fst_uniform : (unif (NonDH F)).map (fun t => t.1.1) = unif F :=
  unif_map_of_equiv_prod
    ((shiftEquivNe F).symm.trans ((Equiv.prodComm _ _).trans (Equiv.prodAssoc F F Fˣ)))
    _ fun _ => rfl

/-- **The reduction with public key, random case**: on a random non-Diffie–Hellman triple,
`reductionPk` produces exactly the random PRF experiment including a uniform public key that is
independent of both tables. -/
theorem reductionPk_rand (g : G) :
    (ddhRandNe F g).bind (reductionPk F g) = (prfRandPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)) := by
  have h : (reductionPk F g ∘ fun t : NonDH F => (t.1.1 • g, t.1.2.1 • g, t.1.2.2 • g)) =
      (fun a : F => (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)).map fun T => (a • g, T)) ∘
        fun t : NonDH F => t.1.1 := by
    funext t
    simp only [Function.comp_apply, reductionPk_eq, reduction_of_nonDH g t.2]
  unfold ddhRandNe
  rw [PMF.bind_map, h, ← PMF.bind_map, nonDH_fst_uniform]
  unfold prfRand prfRandPk
  simp only [PMF.map_comp]
  exact unif_bind_map fun (a : F) (hu : (M → F) × (M → F)) =>
    ((a • g, (fun m => hu.1 m • g, fun m => hu.2 m • g)) : G × Tables M G)

/-- Random worlds coincide, with public key. -/
theorem prfRandPk_bind_eq (g : G) (D : G × Tables M G → PMF β) :
    (prfRandPk F g).bind D = (ddhRandNe F g).bind (ddhDistinguisherPk F g D) := by
  rw [← reductionPk_rand, PMF.bind_bind]
  rfl

/-- PRF advantage with public key = advantage of the explicit distinguisher against the
`c ≠ a * b` variant of DDH, exactly. -/
theorem prfPkAdvantage_eq_ddhNeAdvantage (g : G) (D : G × Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    prfPkAdvantage F g D = ddhNeAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisherPk F g D) := by
  unfold prfPkAdvantage ddhNeAdvantage
  rw [prfRealPk_bind_eq, prfRandPk_bind_eq]

/-- **The PRF reduces to textbook DDH even when the distinguisher sees the public key
`k • g`.**  For every distinguisher `D` of (public key, hash table, value table), its advantage
in telling the real values from independent uniform multiples of `g` (independent uniform group
elements when `g` is a generator, `prfRandPk_eq_unif`) is at most the textbook-DDH advantage of
`ddhDistinguisherPk F g D` plus `1/|F|`.

Same caveats as `prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add`: this is the reduction only; DDH is not
proved or assumed; `ddhDistinguisherPk F g D` builds `2 * |M|` table entries, so for a large
message space use `prfPkAdvantage_restrict_le` or `prfPkAdvantage_oracle_le`; and `D` is NOT
given an oracle `a ↦ k • a` on group elements of its choice, so a party A that deviates from the
protocol is not covered (section L4 of `Limitations.lean`, `KeyPrivacy.lean`).  The public key
lives in the same group as the signatures and there is no pairing; with the pairing layer of
`PublicVerify.lean` signatures are publicly verifiable and hence not pseudorandom given the
public key. -/
theorem prfPkAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add (g : G) (D : G × Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    prfPkAdvantage F g D ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisherPk F g D) + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  rw [prfPkAdvantage_eq_ddhNeAdvantage]
  exact ddhNeAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g _

omit [DecidableEq F] in
/-- A distinguisher that ignores the public key has the same advantage in both games: the game
without public key is a special case of the game with public key. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_eq_prfPkAdvantage (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g D = prfPkAdvantage F g (D ∘ Prod.snd) := by
  unfold prfAdvantage prfPkAdvantage
  rw [← PMF.bind_map, ← PMF.bind_map, prfRealPk_map_snd, prfRandPk_map_snd]

end PublicKey

/-! ## H. Locality: table entries that are not read need not be built

The reduction of section A builds two complete tables, which costs work linear in `|M|`.  This
section proves that restricting the tables to any family of distinct messages (an injection
`ι : S → M`) commutes with everything: with the reduction, with both PRF experiments, and hence
with advantages.  So a distinguisher that reads only the entries indexed by `S` is served by the
reduction run over the message space `S`: `2 * |S|` double scalar multiplications, independent of
`|M|`.  This is lazy sampling for a set of queries fixed in advance; queries that depend on
earlier answers are the subject of section I. -/

section Locality

variable {S : Type y}

/-- Restrict a pair of tables to the messages `ι s`, `s : S`: forget every other entry. -/
def restrictTables (ι : S → M) (T : Tables M G) : Tables S G := (T.1 ∘ ι, T.2 ∘ ι)

variable [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]
  [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] {β : Type x}

omit [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] [Fintype F] in
/-- Restriction commutes with the deterministic core: the restricted tables are computed from the
restricted coins alone. -/
theorem reductionCore_restrict (ι : S → M) (g : G) (inst : G × G × G)
    (xy : (M → F) × (M → F)) :
    restrictTables ι (reductionCore g inst xy) = reductionCore g inst (xy.1 ∘ ι, xy.2 ∘ ι) :=
  rfl

/-- **The reduction is local.**  Running the reduction over the whole message space `M` and then
keeping only the entries at the (distinct) messages `ι s` is EXACTLY the reduction run over the
message space `S`.  Entries that are not looked at need not be computed, and the entries that are
looked at do not depend on the size of `M`. -/
theorem reduction_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) :
    (reduction F g inst : PMF (Tables M G)).map (restrictTables ι) =
      (reduction F g inst : PMF (Tables S G)) := by
  have h := unif_map_prodMap _ _ (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
    (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
  unfold reduction
  rw [PMF.map_comp, ← h, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- The real PRF experiment is local: its restriction to the messages `ι s` is the real PRF
experiment over the message space `S` (same key distribution). -/
theorem prfReal_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G) :
    (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)).map (restrictTables ι) =
      (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables S G)) := by
  have h := unif_map_prodMap (id : F → F) _ (PMF.map_id _)
    (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
  unfold prfReal
  rw [PMF.map_comp, ← h, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- The random PRF experiment is local: its restriction to the messages `ι s` is the random PRF
experiment over the message space `S`. -/
theorem prfRand_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G) :
    (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)).map (restrictTables ι) =
      (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables S G)) := by
  have h := unif_map_prodMap _ _ (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
    (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
  unfold prfRand
  rw [PMF.map_comp, ← h, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- **The big and the small DDH distinguisher are the same function.**  If `D` only reads the
entries at the messages `ι s` (i.e. `D = D₀ ∘ restrictTables ι`), then "run the reduction over
all of `M`, then `D`" has, on every instance, exactly the output distribution of "run the
reduction over `S`, then `D₀`".  The right-hand side does `2 * |S|` double scalar multiplications
and one run of `D₀`, whatever the size of `M`. -/
theorem ddhDistinguisher_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G)
    (D₀ : Tables S G → PMF β) :
    ddhDistinguisher F g (D₀ ∘ restrictTables (M := M) ι) = ddhDistinguisher F g D₀ := by
  funext inst
  rw [ddhDistinguisher_apply, ddhDistinguisher_apply, ← reduction_restrict ι hι, PMF.bind_map]

/-- A distinguisher that only reads the entries at the messages `ι s` has, over the big message
space `M`, exactly the PRF advantage of its core `D₀` over the small message space `S`. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G)
    (D₀ : Tables S G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g (D₀ ∘ restrictTables (M := M) ι) = prfAdvantage F g D₀ := by
  unfold prfAdvantage
  rw [← prfReal_restrict (F := F) ι hι g, ← prfRand_restrict (F := F) ι hι g, PMF.bind_map,
    PMF.bind_map]

/-- **Lazy sampling for a fixed set of queries.**  Let `D` be a distinguisher over an arbitrarily
large (finite) message space `M` that reads only the hash and value entries at the distinct
messages `ι s`, `s : S` (a non-adaptive adversary with `|S|` queries).  Then its PRF advantage is
at most the textbook-DDH advantage of `ddhDistinguisher F g D₀` plus `1/|F|`, where the DDH
distinguisher is built over the message space `S` only: it does `2 * |S|` double scalar
multiplications and one run of `D₀`, independent of `|M|`.

What this does NOT cover: a distinguisher whose choice of messages depends on earlier answers
(see section I for those), and the same non-claims as `prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add` (DDH
is an assumption; "efficient" is by inspection). -/
theorem prfAdvantage_restrict_le [DecidableEq F] (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G)
    (D₀ : Tables S G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g (D₀ ∘ restrictTables (M := M) ι) ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisher F g D₀) + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  rw [prfAdvantage_restrict ι hι]
  exact prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g D₀

omit [Fintype S] [DecidableEq S] in
/-- The same for a finite SET `Q` of queried messages: a distinguisher that reads only the
entries at the messages in `Q` is bounded through a reduction that builds `2 * |Q|` entries. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_finset_le [DecidableEq F] (Q : Finset M) (g : G)
    (D₀ : Tables Q G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g (D₀ ∘ restrictTables (M := M) (Subtype.val : Q → M)) ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisher F g D₀) + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) :=
  prfAdvantage_restrict_le Subtype.val Subtype.val_injective g D₀

/-- The reduction with public key is local as well. -/
theorem reductionPk_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G)
    (inst : G × G × G) :
    (reductionPk F g inst : PMF (G × Tables M G)).map (Prod.map id (restrictTables ι)) =
      (reductionPk F g inst : PMF (G × Tables S G)) := by
  rw [reductionPk_eq, reductionPk_eq, ← reduction_restrict ι hι, PMF.map_comp, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- The real PRF experiment with public key is local. -/
theorem prfRealPk_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G) :
    (prfRealPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)).map (Prod.map id (restrictTables ι)) =
      (prfRealPk F g : PMF (G × Tables S G)) := by
  have h := unif_map_prodMap (id : F → F) _ (PMF.map_id _)
    (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
  unfold prfRealPk
  rw [PMF.map_comp, ← h, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- The random PRF experiment with public key is local. -/
theorem prfRandPk_restrict (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι) (g : G) :
    (prfRandPk F g : PMF (G × Tables M G)).map (Prod.map id (restrictTables ι)) =
      (prfRandPk F g : PMF (G × Tables S G)) := by
  have h := unif_map_prodMap (id : F → F) _ (PMF.map_id _)
    (unif_map_prodMap _ _ (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι)
      (unif_map_comp_injective (K := F) ι hι))
  unfold prfRandPk
  rw [PMF.map_comp, ← h, PMF.map_comp]
  rfl

/-- **Lazy sampling for a fixed set of queries, with the public key in view.**  As
`prfAdvantage_restrict_le`, for a distinguisher that sees the public key `k • g` and reads only
the table entries at the messages `ι s`. -/
theorem prfPkAdvantage_restrict_le [DecidableEq F] (ι : S → M) (hι : Function.Injective ι)
    (g : G) (D₀ : G × Tables S G → PMF Bool) :
    prfPkAdvantage F g (D₀ ∘ Prod.map id (restrictTables (M := M) ι)) ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (ddhDistinguisherPk F g D₀) + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  have h : prfPkAdvantage F g (D₀ ∘ Prod.map id (restrictTables (M := M) ι)) =
      prfPkAdvantage F g D₀ := by
    unfold prfPkAdvantage
    rw [← prfRealPk_restrict (F := F) ι hι g, ← prfRandPk_restrict (F := F) ι hι g,
      PMF.bind_map, PMF.bind_map]
  rw [h]
  exact prfPkAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g D₀

end Locality

/-! ## I. Adaptive oracle adversaries and lazy sampling

The usual PRF adversary does not receive tables: it has oracle access, asks for one message at a
time, and chooses each message after seeing the earlier answers.  This section models such an
adversary as a decision tree (`OracleAdv`), observes that it is a special case of a table
distinguisher (`OracleAdv.run`, so sections D to G apply to it), and proves that the DDH
distinguisher "run the reduction on complete tables, then the adversary" is THE SAME FUNCTION as
the lazy distinguisher that draws coins and computes table entries only for the messages the
adversary actually asks about (`lazyDistinguisher_eq`). -/

section Adaptive

/-- **An adaptive oracle distinguisher**, as a decision tree.

* `done out`: stop and output a sample of `out`;
* `query m next`: ask the oracle about message `m`, receive the pair
  (hash `H m`, function value at `m`), and continue as `next` of that pair.

The next message may depend on all earlier answers, and so may the number of queries.  One query
returns the hash AND the value; an adversary interested in only one of them ignores the other.
A tree is deterministic between queries; internal randomness is covered by drawing a random tape
first and then choosing the tree (`prfAdvantage_randomizedOracle_le`).  No notion of running
time is attached to a tree. -/
inductive OracleAdv (M : Type w) (G : Type v) (β : Type x) : Type (max v w x)
  /-- Stop and output a sample of `out`. -/
  | done (out : PMF β) : OracleAdv M G β
  /-- Ask about message `m`; continue with `next (H m, value at m)`. -/
  | query (m : M) (next : G × G → OracleAdv M G β) : OracleAdv M G β

/-- Complete the coins drawn so far (`σ`, defined at the messages already queried) by the coins
`c` at all other messages. -/
def fillCoins {V : Type*} (σ : M → Option V) (c : M → V) : M → V := fun m => (σ m).getD (c m)

/-- Overwriting the fresh coin at a message that has not been queried yet is the same as
recording that coin as drawn. -/
theorem fillCoins_update {V : Type*} [DecidableEq M] (σ : M → Option V) (c : M → V) {m : M}
    (hσ : σ m = none) (v : V) :
    fillCoins σ (Function.update c m v) = fillCoins (Function.update σ m (some v)) c := by
  funext m'
  by_cases h : m' = m
  · subst h
    simp [fillCoins, hσ]
  · simp [fillCoins, h]

/-- The reduction's tables written with one pair of coins per message (`M → F × F` instead of
`(M → F) × (M → F)`); see `reductionCore_entry`. -/
def coinTables [Field F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] (g : G) (inst : G × G × G)
    (c : M → F × F) : Tables M G :=
  (fun m => (reductionEntry g inst (c m)).1, fun m => (reductionEntry g inst (c m)).2)

namespace OracleAdv

variable {β : Type x}

/-- Run an oracle adversary against complete tables: each query `m` is answered by the two table
entries at `m`.  This turns an oracle adversary into a table distinguisher, which is the precise
sense in which "handing over both complete tables subsumes every adaptive oracle adversary". -/
def run : OracleAdv M G β → Tables M G → PMF β
  | done out, _ => out
  | query m next, T => (next (T.1 m, T.2 m)).run T

variable (F) [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [DecidableEq M]

/-- **The lazy reduction.**  Run the oracle adversary on a DDH instance `inst = (A, B, C)`,
answering each query from `reductionEntry`: a message asked for the first time gets a fresh
uniform pair of coins `(x, y)` and the answer `(x • B + y • g, x • C + y • A)`; the coins are
remembered in `σ`, and a repeated query gets the same answer again.

Per DISTINCT message on the path actually taken: one pair of coins and two double scalar
multiplications.  Nothing is computed for messages that are not queried, so the work does not
depend on `|M|`; indeed the definition does not assume that `M` is finite (it needs only
decidable equality on `M`, to recognise repeated queries).  That the work is small is by
inspection: Lean has no cost model here. -/
noncomputable def lazyRun (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) :
    OracleAdv M G β → (M → Option (F × F)) → PMF β
  | done out, _ => out
  | query m next, σ =>
    match σ m with
    | some xy => (next (reductionEntry g inst xy)).lazyRun g inst σ
    | none => (unif (F × F)).bind fun xy =>
        (next (reductionEntry g inst xy)).lazyRun g inst (Function.update σ m (some xy))

variable {F} [Fintype M]

/-- The invariant behind lazy sampling: with coins `σ` already drawn, the lazy run is the run
against the complete tables obtained by drawing all remaining coins uniformly up front. -/
theorem lazyRun_eq_bind_fillCoins (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) (A : OracleAdv M G β)
    (σ : M → Option (F × F)) :
    A.lazyRun F g inst σ =
      (unif (M → F × F)).bind fun c => A.run (coinTables g inst (fillCoins σ c)) := by
  induction A generalizing σ with
  | done out => exact (unif_bind_const out).symm
  | query m next ih =>
    rw [lazyRun]
    split
    · rename_i xy hσ
      rw [ih]
      congr 1
      funext c
      simp only [run, coinTables, fillCoins, hσ, Option.getD_some]
    · rename_i hσ
      simp only [ih]
      rw [← unif_prod_bind fun p : (F × F) × (M → F × F) =>
          (next (reductionEntry g inst p.1)).run
            (coinTables g inst (fillCoins (Function.update σ m (some p.1)) p.2)),
        ← unif_map_update (K := F × F) m, PMF.bind_map]
      congr 1
      funext p
      simp only [Function.comp_apply, run, coinTables, fillCoins_update σ p.2 hσ p.1]
      simp [fillCoins]

/-- **Lazy sampling is exact.**  Starting with no coins drawn, the lazy run of an adaptive oracle
adversary on a DDH instance has EXACTLY the output distribution of: run the reduction to build
both complete tables, then run the adversary against them.

An equality of distributions for every instance, every module `G` and every `g`.  It says nothing
about hardness; it says that the cheap experiment and the expensive one are the same. -/
theorem lazyRun_eq_reduction (g : G) (inst : G × G × G) (A : OracleAdv M G β) :
    A.lazyRun F g inst (fun _ => none) = (reduction F g inst).bind A.run := by
  have h : (reduction F g inst : PMF (Tables M G)) = (unif (M → F × F)).map (coinTables g inst) :=
    (unif_map_eq_of_equiv (Equiv.arrowProdEquivProdArrow M (fun _ => F) (fun _ => F))
      (coinTables g inst) (reductionCore g inst) fun _ => rfl).symm
  rw [lazyRun_eq_bind_fillCoins, h, PMF.bind_map]
  rfl

end OracleAdv

variable [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]
  {β : Type x}

variable (F) in
/-- **The lazy DDH distinguisher** built from an adaptive oracle adversary `A`: on input a
triple, run `A`, answering its queries by lazy sampling (`OracleAdv.lazyRun`, starting with no
coins drawn).  Its work is one run of `A` plus two double scalar multiplications per distinct
query, with no finiteness assumption on `M`; it takes group elements only, no key and no
discrete logarithm. -/
noncomputable def lazyDistinguisher (g : G) (A : OracleAdv M G β) : G × G × G → PMF β :=
  fun inst => A.lazyRun F g inst fun _ => none

/-- The lazy distinguisher IS the table-based distinguisher of section A applied to `A.run`
(equal as functions from instances to output distributions).  Hence every statement of sections
D to F about `ddhDistinguisher F g A.run` is a statement about the lazy distinguisher. -/
theorem lazyDistinguisher_eq (g : G) (A : OracleAdv M G β) :
    lazyDistinguisher F g A = ddhDistinguisher F g A.run :=
  funext fun inst => A.lazyRun_eq_reduction g inst

/-- For an adaptive oracle adversary, PRF advantage = advantage of the lazy distinguisher against
the `c ≠ a * b` variant of DDH, exactly. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_oracle_eq [DecidableEq F] (g : G) (A : OracleAdv M G Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g A.run = ddhNeAdvantage F g (lazyDistinguisher F g A) := by
  rw [lazyDistinguisher_eq]
  exact prfAdvantage_eq_ddhNeAdvantage g A.run

/-- **The PRF reduces to textbook DDH for adaptive oracle adversaries, with a lazy reduction.**
For every adaptive oracle adversary `A` (a decision tree that asks for one message at a time and
sees the hash and the function value; no bound on its queries, no efficiency requirement), its
advantage in telling the real function `m ↦ k • H m` from independent uniform multiples of `g`
is at most the textbook-DDH advantage of `lazyDistinguisher F g A` plus `1/|F|`.

The DDH distinguisher here computes two table entries per distinct query of `A`, not `2 * |M|`;
the message space may be astronomically large (it must be finite and have decidable equality).

What this does NOT say: that either advantage is small (that is the DDH assumption for the group
generated by `g`, neither proved nor assumed); anything quantitative about running time (no cost
model; `lazyRun` is short enough to read).  `A` gets the hash/value oracle only: it is NOT given
an oracle `a ↦ k • a` on group elements of its choice, which is what party A of the protocol
has; see the docstring of `prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add`. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_oracle_le [DecidableEq F] (g : G) (A : OracleAdv M G Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g A.run ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (lazyDistinguisher F g A) + 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  rw [lazyDistinguisher_eq]
  exact prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g A.run

/-- **Randomised adaptive adversaries.**  An adversary that also tosses coins is a distribution
`ρ` over random tapes `r` together with a decision tree `A r` for each tape.  The bound of
`prfAdvantage_oracle_le` holds for it, with the lazy distinguisher that draws the tape and then
runs `lazyDistinguisher F g (A r)`. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_randomizedOracle_le [DecidableEq F] {R : Type z} (g : G) (ρ : PMF R)
    (A : R → OracleAdv M G Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g (fun T => ρ.bind fun r => (A r).run T) ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (fun inst => ρ.bind fun r => lazyDistinguisher F g (A r) inst) +
        1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  have h : ddhDistinguisher F g (fun T => ρ.bind fun r => (A r).run T) =
      fun inst => ρ.bind fun r => lazyDistinguisher F g (A r) inst := by
    funext inst
    simp only [lazyDistinguisher_eq, ddhDistinguisher_apply]
    exact PMF.bind_comm _ _ _
  rw [← h]
  exact prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g _

/-- **Adaptive oracle adversaries that also see the public key `k • g`.**  The adversary is a
decision tree `A pk` chosen after seeing the public key `pk`.  Its advantage is at most the
textbook-DDH advantage, plus `1/|F|`, of the lazy distinguisher that passes the first component
of its instance to the adversary as the public key.  Same non-claims as
`prfAdvantage_oracle_le`. -/
theorem prfPkAdvantage_oracle_le [DecidableEq F] (g : G) (A : G → OracleAdv M G Bool) :
    prfPkAdvantage F g (fun pT => (A pT.1).run pT.2) ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (fun inst => lazyDistinguisher F g (A inst.1) inst) +
        1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  have h : ddhDistinguisherPk F g (fun pT : G × Tables M G => (A pT.1).run pT.2) =
      fun inst => lazyDistinguisher F g (A inst.1) inst := by
    funext inst
    rw [lazyDistinguisher_eq]
    show (reductionPk F g inst).bind _ = (reduction F g inst).bind _
    rw [reductionPk_eq, PMF.bind_map]
    rfl
  rw [← h]
  exact prfPkAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g _

/-- **The most general adversary of this file**: randomised, adaptive, and given the public key.
The adversary draws a random tape `r` from `ρ`, sees the public key `pk`, and then runs the
decision tree `A r pk`.  Its advantage is at most the textbook-DDH advantage, plus `1/|F|`, of
the lazy distinguisher that draws the tape, passes the first component of its instance as the
public key, and answers the queries by lazy sampling.  Same non-claims as
`prfAdvantage_oracle_le`; in particular the adversary has no oracle `a ↦ k • a`. -/
theorem prfPkAdvantage_randomizedOracle_le [DecidableEq F] {R : Type z} (g : G) (ρ : PMF R)
    (A : R → G → OracleAdv M G Bool) :
    prfPkAdvantage F g (fun pT => ρ.bind fun r => (A r pT.1).run pT.2) ≤
      ddhAdvantage F g (fun inst => ρ.bind fun r => lazyDistinguisher F g (A r inst.1) inst) +
        1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  have h : ddhDistinguisherPk F g
        (fun pT : G × Tables M G => ρ.bind fun r => (A r pT.1).run pT.2) =
      fun inst => ρ.bind fun r => lazyDistinguisher F g (A r inst.1) inst := by
    funext inst
    simp only [lazyDistinguisher_eq, ddhDistinguisher_apply]
    show (reductionPk F g inst).bind _ = _
    rw [reductionPk_eq, PMF.bind_map]
    exact PMF.bind_comm _ _ _
  rw [← h]
  exact prfPkAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add g _

end Adaptive

/-! ## J. What unbounded distinguishers achieve; non-degeneracy

Sections C to I are identities between experiments.  They would be empty if the two worlds of a
game had been defined so as to coincide.  They have not, but HOW far apart the two PRF worlds are
depends on the number of messages:

* with ONE message, every distinguisher whatsoever has advantage at most `1/|F|`
  (`prfAdvantage_le_of_unique`): a single value `k • H m` is statistically pseudorandom, and
  the bare inequality `prfReal_ne_prfRand` carries no cryptographic weight there;
* with at least TWO messages, an explicit brute-force test has advantage at least `1 - 1/|F|`
  (`prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge_of_nontrivial`), so there pseudorandomness of the function can
  only be computational. -/

section Separation

/-- Two different distributions are told apart with positive advantage by some test, namely the
indicator of an outcome on which they differ.  (Nothing is said about the size of the advantage
or about the cost of the test.) -/
theorem exists_advantage_pos_of_ne {α : Type*} {p q : PMF α} (h : p ≠ q) :
    ∃ D : α → PMF Bool, 0 < advantage (p.bind D) (q.bind D) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ : ∃ a, p a ≠ q a := by
    by_contra hcon
    exact h (PMF.ext fun a => not_not.mp fun hne => hcon ⟨a, hne⟩)
  have key : ∀ r : PMF α, (r.bind fun x => PMF.pure (decide (x = a))) true = r a := by
    intro r
    rw [PMF.bind_apply, tsum_eq_single a]
    · simp
    · intro x hx
      simp [hx]
  refine ⟨fun x => PMF.pure (decide (x = a)), ?_⟩
  unfold advantage
  rw [key, key]
  refine abs_pos.mpr (sub_ne_zero.mpr fun hEq => ha ?_)
  exact (ENNReal.toReal_eq_toReal_iff' (PMF.apply_ne_top _ _) (PMF.apply_ne_top _ _)).mp hEq

/-- If two one-bit experiments start with the same uniform sample `a` and continue identically
except when `a = a₀`, then their advantage is at most `1/|α|`, the probability of `a = a₀`. -/
theorem advantage_unif_bind_le_of_eq_off {α : Type*} [Fintype α] [Nonempty α] (a₀ : α)
    (f f' : α → PMF Bool) (h : ∀ a, a ≠ a₀ → f a = f' a) :
    advantage ((unif α).bind f) ((unif α).bind f') ≤ 1 / (Fintype.card α : ℝ) := by
  have hq : (0 : ℝ) < (Fintype.card α : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_pos
  have hsum : ∀ φ : α → PMF Bool, (((unif α).bind φ) true).toReal =
      ∑ a, (Fintype.card α : ℝ)⁻¹ * (φ a true).toReal := by
    intro φ
    rw [unif_bind_apply, ENNReal.toReal_sum fun a _ => ENNReal.mul_ne_top
      (ENNReal.inv_ne_top.mpr (by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_ne_zero)) (PMF.apply_ne_top _ _)]
    simp only [ENNReal.toReal_mul, ENNReal.toReal_inv, ENNReal.toReal_natCast]
  have h1 : |((f a₀) true).toReal - ((f' a₀) true).toReal| ≤ 1 := advantage_le_one (f a₀) (f' a₀)
  unfold advantage
  rw [hsum, hsum, ← Finset.sum_sub_distrib,
    Finset.sum_eq_single a₀ (fun a _ ha => by rw [h a ha, sub_self])
      (fun ha => absurd (Finset.mem_univ a₀) ha),
    ← mul_sub, abs_mul, abs_of_nonneg (inv_nonneg.mpr hq.le), one_div]
  exact mul_le_of_le_one_right (inv_nonneg.mpr hq.le) h1

end Separation

section NonDegenerate

variable [Field F] [Fintype F] [AddCommGroup G] [Module F G] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M]

/-- **The two PRF worlds are not the same distribution.**  If there is at least one message and
`g ≠ 0`, then `prfReal F g ≠ prfRand F g`: the pair "all-zero hash table, value table constantly
`g`" has positive probability in the random world and probability zero in the real one.

This is a WEAK statement and is meant only as a sanity check that the two games have not been
defined so as to coincide.  The witnessing outcome is a single pair of tables (of probability
`|F|^(-2|M|)` in the random world, by counting; that number is a remark, not a theorem of this
file), so by itself the inequality is compatible with the two worlds being statistically
indistinguishable, and for a single message they are (`prfAdvantage_le_of_unique`).  It does
NOT say that pseudorandomness "can only be computational"; the quantitative statement for at
least two messages is `prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge_of_nontrivial`. -/
theorem prfReal_ne_prfRand [Nonempty M] {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)) ≠ prfRand F g := by
  intro h
  have h1 : ((fun _ => 0, fun _ => g) : Tables M G) ∈ (prfRand F g).support := by
    rw [prfRand, PMF.mem_support_map_iff]
    exact ⟨(fun _ => 0, fun _ => 1), mem_support_unif _, by simp⟩
  rw [← h, prfReal, PMF.mem_support_map_iff] at h1
  obtain ⟨⟨k, e⟩, -, hT⟩ := h1
  obtain ⟨m₀⟩ := ‹Nonempty M›
  have e1 : e m₀ • g = 0 := congrFun (congrArg Prod.fst hT) m₀
  have e2 : k • e m₀ • g = g := congrFun (congrArg Prod.snd hT) m₀
  rw [e1, smul_zero] at e2
  exact hg e2.symm

/-- **Some distinguisher has positive PRF advantage** (at least one message, `g ≠ 0`): the PRF
advantage is not identically zero by construction.  The distinguisher is obtained
non-constructively from `prfReal_ne_prfRand` (the indicator of some pair of tables on which the
two worlds differ); nothing here says that it is efficient or that its advantage is large.  For
an explicit test with large advantage see `prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge`. -/
theorem exists_distinguisher_pos [Nonempty M] {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    ∃ D : Tables M G → PMF Bool, 0 < prfAdvantage F g D :=
  exists_advantage_pos_of_ne (prfReal_ne_prfRand hg)

omit [Fintype F] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
variable (F) in
/-- **A brute-force PRF test**: accept iff the value table is a scalar multiple of the hash
table, i.e. iff some key `k : F` explains every entry, `T.2 m = k • T.1 m` for all `m`.

Deciding this by exhaustive search over `k` costs `|F|` table comparisons, which is exponential
in the bit length of the group order: this test is NOT an efficient distinguisher and its
existence does not contradict DDH.  (It is defined with classical decidability.) -/
noncomputable def scalarTest : Tables M G → PMF Bool :=
  fun T => PMF.pure (@decide (∃ k : F, T.2 = k • T.1) (Classical.propDecidable _))

/-- In the real world the brute-force test always accepts (the key is the scalar). -/
theorem prfReal_bind_scalarTest (g : G) :
    (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)).bind (scalarTest F) = PMF.pure true := by
  have h : (scalarTest F ∘ fun kh : F × (M → F) =>
      ((fun m => kh.2 m • g, fun m => kh.1 • (kh.2 m • g)) : Tables M G)) =
        fun _ => PMF.pure true := by
    funext kh
    simp only [Function.comp_apply, scalarTest]
    congr 1
    exact @decide_eq_true _ (Classical.propDecidable _) ⟨kh.1, rfl⟩
  unfold prfReal
  rw [PMF.bind_map, h, unif_bind_const]

/-- In the random world the brute-force test accepts with probability at most `|F| / |F|^|M|`:
among the `|F|^|M| * |F|^|M|` pairs of exponent tables at most `|F| * |F|^|M|` have the second
table a scalar multiple of the first. -/
theorem prfRand_bind_scalarTest_le {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    ((prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)).bind (scalarTest F)) true ≤
      (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞) / (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞) ^ Fintype.card M := by
  have hq0 : (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞) ^ Fintype.card M ≠ 0 :=
    pow_ne_zero _ (by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_ne_zero)
  have hqt : (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞) ^ Fintype.card M ≠ ⊤ :=
    ENNReal.pow_ne_top (ENNReal.natCast_ne_top _)
  have h := unif_bind_pure_true_le (α := (M → F) × (M → F)) (κ := F × (M → F))
    (fun hu => @decide (∃ k : F, (fun m => hu.2 m • g) = k • fun m => hu.1 m • g)
      (Classical.propDecidable _))
    (fun p => (p.2, fun m => p.1 * p.2 m))
    (by
      rintro ⟨h', u⟩ hdec
      obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := @of_decide_eq_true _ (Classical.propDecidable _) hdec
      refine ⟨(k, h'), Prod.ext rfl (funext fun m => ?_)⟩
      have hm : u m • g = (k * h' m) • g := by
        rw [mul_smul]
        exact congrFun hk m
      exact (smul_left_injective F hg hm).symm)
  unfold prfRand
  rw [PMF.bind_map]
  refine (le_of_eq ?_).trans (h.trans (le_of_eq ?_))
  · rfl
  · simp only [Fintype.card_prod, Fintype.card_fun, Nat.cast_mul, Nat.cast_pow]
    exact ENNReal.mul_div_mul_right _ _ hq0 hqt

/-- **An unbounded distinguisher wins.**  If `g ≠ 0`, the brute-force test `scalarTest F` has PRF
advantage at least `1 - |F| / |F|^|M|`.  With two or more messages this is at least `1 - 1/|F|`
(`prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge_of_nontrivial`), and it tends to `1` as the number of messages
grows.  With one message the bound is `0` and says nothing, as it must
(`prfAdvantage_le_of_unique`).

This is a statement about an inefficient test.  It shows that the PRF property cannot hold
against unbounded distinguishers once there are two messages; it does not bear on DDH. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    1 - (Fintype.card F : ℝ) / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) ^ Fintype.card M ≤
      prfAdvantage F g (scalarTest F : Tables M G → PMF Bool) := by
  have h1 := prfRand_bind_scalarTest_le (F := F) (M := M) hg
  have hne : (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞) / (Fintype.card F : ℝ≥0∞) ^ Fintype.card M ≠ ⊤ :=
    ENNReal.div_ne_top (ENNReal.natCast_ne_top _)
      (pow_ne_zero _ (by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_ne_zero))
  have h2 : (((prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)).bind (scalarTest F)) true).toReal ≤
      (Fintype.card F : ℝ) / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) ^ Fintype.card M := by
    have h3 := ENNReal.toReal_mono hne h1
    rwa [ENNReal.toReal_div, ENNReal.toReal_pow, ENNReal.toReal_natCast] at h3
  unfold prfAdvantage advantage
  rw [prfReal_bind_scalarTest, PMF.pure_apply_self, ENNReal.toReal_one]
  exact (sub_le_sub_left h2 1).trans (le_abs_self _)

/-- **With at least two messages the PRF property is at best computational.**  If `g ≠ 0` and
the message space has two distinct messages, the brute-force test `scalarTest F` tells the real
function from the random world with advantage at least `1 - 1/|F|`.

So no bound on the advantage of ALL distinguishers can hold; any security statement must restrict
to efficient ones, which is what the DDH assumption is about.  Not claimed: that this test is the
best one, or that it is efficient (it is not). -/
theorem prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge_of_nontrivial [Nontrivial M] {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    1 - 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) ≤ prfAdvantage F g (scalarTest F : Tables M G → PMF Bool) := by
  refine le_trans ?_ (prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge hg)
  have hq1 : (1 : ℝ) ≤ (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast Fintype.card_pos
  have hq : (0 : ℝ) < (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := lt_of_lt_of_le one_pos hq1
  have hM : 2 ≤ Fintype.card M := Fintype.one_lt_card
  have h : (Fintype.card F : ℝ) / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) ^ Fintype.card M ≤
      1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
    rw [div_le_div_iff₀ (pow_pos hq _) hq, one_mul, ← pow_two]
    exact pow_le_pow_right₀ hq1 hM
  linarith

/-- **With a single message the value IS statistically pseudorandom.**  If the message space has
exactly one element, then EVERY distinguisher `D`, efficient or not, has PRF advantage at most
`1/|F|`: for a nonzero hash exponent `h` the map `k ↦ k * h` is a bijection, so the two worlds
differ only on the event `h = 0`.

This is why `prfReal_ne_prfRand` alone proves nothing about computational versus
information-theoretic security, and why the Diffie–Hellman assumption only enters with the
second message.  No hypothesis on `g`. -/
theorem prfAdvantage_le_of_unique [Unique M] (g : G) (D : Tables M G → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage F g D ≤ 1 / (Fintype.card F : ℝ) := by
  let T : F → F → Tables M G := fun h u => (fun _ => h • g, fun _ => u • g)
  have hreal : (prfReal F g : PMF (Tables M G)).bind D =
      (unif F).bind fun h => (unif F).bind fun k => D (T h (k * h)) := by
    unfold prfReal
    rw [PMF.bind_map, ← unif_prod_bind fun p : F × F => D (T p.1 (p.2 * p.1))]
    refine unif_bind_eq_of_equiv
      ((Equiv.prodCongr (Equiv.refl F) (Equiv.funUnique M F)).trans (Equiv.prodComm F F)) _ _ ?_
    rintro ⟨k, h⟩
    show D (T (h default) (k * h default)) = D (fun m => h m • g, fun m => k • (h m • g))
    congr 1
    refine Prod.ext (funext fun m => ?_) (funext fun m => ?_)
    · show h default • g = h m • g
      rw [Unique.eq_default m]
    · show (k * h default) • g = k • (h m • g)
      rw [Unique.eq_default m, mul_smul]
  have hrand : (prfRand F g : PMF (Tables M G)).bind D =
      (unif F).bind fun h => (unif F).bind fun u => D (T h u) := by
    unfold prfRand
    rw [PMF.bind_map, ← unif_prod_bind fun p : F × F => D (T p.1 p.2)]
    refine unif_bind_eq_of_equiv
      (Equiv.prodCongr (Equiv.funUnique M F) (Equiv.funUnique M F)) _ _ ?_
    rintro ⟨h, u⟩
    show D (T (h default) (u default)) = D (fun m => h m • g, fun m => u m • g)
    congr 1
    refine Prod.ext (funext fun m => ?_) (funext fun m => ?_)
    · show h default • g = h m • g
      rw [Unique.eq_default m]
    · show u default • g = u m • g
      rw [Unique.eq_default m]
  unfold prfAdvantage
  rw [hreal, hrand]
  refine advantage_unif_bind_le_of_eq_off (0 : F) _ _ fun h hh => ?_
  exact unif_bind_eq_of_equiv (Equiv.mulRight₀ h hh) _ _ fun _ => rfl

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- The real DDH world and the `c ≠ a * b` world are different distributions when `g ≠ 0`: the
triple `(0, 0, g)` is a possible non-DH triple but not a DH triple.  (A sanity check only; like
`prfReal_ne_prfRand` it says nothing about how far apart the two worlds are.) -/
theorem ddhReal_ne_ddhRandNe [DecidableEq F] {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    ddhReal F g ≠ ddhRandNe F g := by
  intro h
  have h1 : ((0, 0, g) : G × G × G) ∈ (ddhRandNe F g).support := by
    rw [ddhRandNe, PMF.mem_support_map_iff]
    exact ⟨⟨(0, 0, 1), by simp⟩, mem_support_unif _, by simp⟩
  rw [← h, ddhReal, PMF.mem_support_map_iff] at h1
  obtain ⟨⟨a, b⟩, -, hT⟩ := h1
  have e1 : a • g = 0 := congrArg Prod.fst hT
  have e3 : (a * b) • g = g := congrArg (fun t => t.2.2) hT
  rw [mul_comm, mul_smul, e1, smul_zero] at e3
  exact hg e3.symm

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- The real DDH world and the TEXTBOOK random DDH world are different distributions when
`g ≠ 0` (same witness `(0, 0, g)`).  The headline theorems are stated against textbook DDH, so
this is the non-degeneracy statement that matters for them. -/
theorem ddhReal_ne_ddhRand {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) : ddhReal F g ≠ ddhRand F g := by
  intro h
  have h1 : ((0, 0, g) : G × G × G) ∈ (ddhRand F g).support := by
    rw [ddhRand, PMF.mem_support_map_iff]
    exact ⟨(0, 0, 1), mem_support_unif _, by simp⟩
  rw [← h, ddhReal, PMF.mem_support_map_iff] at h1
  obtain ⟨⟨a, b⟩, -, hT⟩ := h1
  have e1 : a • g = 0 := congrArg Prod.fst hT
  have e3 : (a * b) • g = g := congrArg (fun t => t.2.2) hT
  rw [mul_comm, mul_smul, e1, smul_zero] at e3
  exact hg e3.symm

omit [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] in
/-- Some (in general inefficient) distinguisher has positive textbook-DDH advantage when
`g ≠ 0`: `ddhAdvantage` is not identically zero by construction.  Nothing is claimed about
efficient distinguishers; that their advantage is negligible is the DDH assumption. -/
theorem exists_ddhDistinguisher_pos {g : G} (hg : g ≠ 0) :
    ∃ D' : G × G × G → PMF Bool, 0 < ddhAdvantage F g D' :=
  exists_advantage_pos_of_ne (ddhReal_ne_ddhRand hg)

end NonDegenerate

/-! ## K. Non-vacuity: a model of all hypotheses -/

section NonVacuity

/-- The model `F = G = ZMod p`, `g = 1` (`p` prime) satisfies the generator hypothesis used in
section B.  (The reduction theorems of this file need no hypothesis on `g` at all.) -/
theorem isGenerator_one_zmod (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] : IsGenerator (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) :=
  ⟨one_ne_zero, fun c => ⟨c, by simp⟩⟩

/-- The main theorem instantiated in that model, with four messages. -/
example (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (D : Tables (Fin 4) (ZMod p) → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) D ≤
      ddhAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) (ddhDistinguisher (ZMod p) 1 D) +
        1 / (Fintype.card (ZMod p) : ℝ) :=
  prfAdvantage_le_ddhAdvantage_add 1 D

/-- The adaptive (lazy) form instantiated in that model, with `2^128` messages: far too many to
tabulate, which is the point of section I. -/
example (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (A : OracleAdv (Fin (2 ^ 128)) (ZMod p) Bool) :
    prfAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) A.run ≤
      ddhAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) (lazyDistinguisher (ZMod p) 1 A) +
        1 / (Fintype.card (ZMod p) : ℝ) :=
  prfAdvantage_oracle_le 1 A

/-- Lazy sampling for two fixed queries out of `2^128` messages, in that model: the DDH
distinguisher on the right is built over the two-element message space `Fin 2`. -/
example (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (D₀ : Tables (Fin 2) (ZMod p) → PMF Bool) :
    prfAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p)
        (D₀ ∘ restrictTables (M := Fin (2 ^ 128)) (Fin.castLE (by norm_num))) ≤
      ddhAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) (ddhDistinguisher (ZMod p) 1 D₀) +
        1 / (Fintype.card (ZMod p) : ℝ) :=
  prfAdvantage_restrict_le _ (Fin.castLE_injective _) 1 D₀

/-- In that model the random world is the uniform pair of tables and the real world is the
honest random-oracle experiment. -/
example (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] :
    (prfRand (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) : PMF (Tables (Fin 4) (ZMod p))) = unif _ ∧
      (prfReal (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) : PMF (Tables (Fin 4) (ZMod p))) =
        (unif (ZMod p)).bind fun k =>
          (unif (Fin 4 → ZMod p)).map fun H => (H, fun m => prf H k m) :=
  ⟨prfRand_eq_unif (isGenerator_one_zmod p), prfReal_eq_randomOracle (isGenerator_one_zmod p)⟩

/-- In that model the two PRF worlds are far apart for an unbounded test (four messages), and
statistically close for every test when there is a single message. -/
example (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] :
    (1 - 1 / (Fintype.card (ZMod p) : ℝ) ≤
        prfAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p)
          (scalarTest (ZMod p) : Tables (Fin 4) (ZMod p) → PMF Bool)) ∧
      ∀ D : Tables (Fin 1) (ZMod p) → PMF Bool,
        prfAdvantage (ZMod p) (1 : ZMod p) D ≤ 1 / (Fintype.card (ZMod p) : ℝ) :=
  ⟨prfAdvantage_scalarTest_ge_of_nontrivial one_ne_zero, fun D => prfAdvantage_le_of_unique 1 D⟩

end NonVacuity

